First we define what we mean by distance on graphs
#def[Distance Function][
  Let $G = (V,E)$ be a connected, simple graph. A _distance function_ on $G$ is a map $ell: E -> RR_+$ such that the triangle inequality holds, i.e. for all $u,v,w in V$ with $(u,v),(v,w),(u,w) in E$ it holds
  $ d(u, w) <= d(u,v) + d(v, w) $
]

#lem[Extending to a Metric][
  Given a graph $G=(V,E)$ with distance function $ell$, we can extend $ell$ to a metric $overline(ell)$ on the vertex set $V$ by taking the shortest path distance. That is,
  $ "dist"_ell (u,w) := min_((u,v_1),(v_1,v_2),...,(v_k,w) in E) sum_(i=0)^k ell(v_i, v_(i+1)), quad u,v in V $
  defines a metric on $V$ with $"dist"_ell (u,v) = ell(u,v)$ for $(u,v) in E$.
]


#def[Star Graph][
  A _star graph_ is a graph with one central vertex $v^*$ and $q in NN$ edges $e_q$ of length $ell_q > 0$ connecting to leaves $v_q$. We call a star _uniform_ if $ell_q = 1$ for all $q$.
]
We can identify $cal(V)$ with $q$ copies of the unit interval $[0,ell_q]$ all glued together at $0$. The distance between two points on different edges is the sum of their distances to the center.

In the following we assume that we are given a finite metric tree $G$. Furthermore we assume that the kernel functions $K_i = K$ are identical.


