#def[Metric Graphs, alternative definition][
  Let $G = (V,E)$ be a connected, simple graph with a length function $ell: E -> RR_+$ fulfilling the triangle inequality. We assume here that $E subs V times V$ is represented as a symmetric relation on $V$, and that $ell$ is a symmetric function on $E$.\
  We identify each edge $e=(u, v) in E$ with a copy of the open interval $(0, ell(e))$, and identify the endpoints $0$ and $ell(e)$ with the vertices $u$ and $v$ respectively. Consider the disjoint union of these intervals:
  $ P := union.sq.big_(e in E) [0, ell(e)] $
  where we denote the point corresponding to $e in E$ and $t in [0, ell(e)]$ by $scp(e,t)$.
  
   define an equivalence relation $~$ on $P$ generated by the rules:
  $
  scp((u,v),t) &~ scp((v,u),ell(u,v)-t) quad quad&& ("reversal") \
  scp((u,v), 0) &~ scp((u,w),0) && ("vertex identification") \ 
  scp((u,v), t) &~ scp((u,v), t) && ("reflexivity") \
  $
  for all $(u,v),(u,w) in E$ and $t in [0, ell(u,v)]$. \
  Define $Gamma := P slash ~$ as the quotient space. Equipping the intervals with the euclidean distance $d_e (scp(e,t), scp(e,t')) := abs(t-t')$ induces a distance function on the edge set 
  $ union.big.sq_(e in E)([0, ell(e)] times [0, ell(e)]) slash ~ quad subs quad Gamma times Gamma $
  $  $ 
  We can extend this to a metric $d$ on $Gamma$ by using the shortest path distances.
  The resulting metric space $(Gamma, d)$ is called the _metric graph_ induced by $G$ and $ell$.
]

Note that the orientatin $arrow(E, size: #1em)$ was chosen to obtain a unique representation of the edge points. Alternatively, we could use $E$ instead and divide $P$ by the relation 
$ scp((u,v), t) ~ scp((v,u), (d(u,v)-t)) $


#def[Metric Graph][
