#def[Regular Center Configuration][
  Let $x = (x_1,...,x_p) in cal(V)^p$ be a placement of $p$ centers on the metric tree $G$. We call $x$ a _regular configuration_ if:
  + No center coincides with a graph vertex: $x_i in cal(E)$ (interior of edges) for all $i$.
  + The removal of $x$ yields exactly $k$ connected components $cal(V)_1,...,cal(V)_k$ with $k >= 2$ (no singleton components after removal).
  + Each component contains at least one graph vertex (non-degenerate Voronoi regions).
]

The set of all regular configurations on $cal(V)$ is denoted $cal(X)_"reg" subs cal(V)^p$.

#lem[Minimum over a Fixed Compact Set is Continuous][
  Let $X$ be a metric space, $T$ a compact metric space, and $f: X times T -> RR$ continuous. Define
  $
    v(x) := min_(t in T) f(x,t).
  $
  Then $v: X -> RR$ is continuous.
]

#pf[
  Let $x_n -> x$. Choose $t_n in T$ with $v(x_n) = f(x_n,t_n)$.
  Since $T$ is compact, after passing to a subsequence we may assume $t_n -> t_* in T$.
  By continuity of $f$,
  $
    liminf_(n->infinity) v(x_n)
    = liminf_(n->infinity) f(x_n,t_n)
    = f(x,t_*)
    >= v(x).
  $
  On the other hand, for any fixed minimizer $hat(t) in arg min_(t in T) f(x,t)$,
  $
    limsup_(n->infinity) v(x_n)
    <= limsup_(n->infinity) f(x_n,hat(t))
    = f(x,hat(t))
    = v(x).
  $
  Hence $v(x_n) -> v(x)$, so $v$ is continuous. $square$
]

#lem[Continuity of Valley Heights under Regular Placement][
  Let $x^0 in cal(X)_"reg"$ and fix a component index $j$.
  Then there exist a neighborhood $U$ of $x^0$ in $cal(X)_"reg"$ and a compact set
  $C_j subs cal(V)$ such that for all $x in U$:
  + $C_j subs cal(V)_j(x)$,
  + $min_(t in cal(V)_j(x)) F(x,t) = min_(t in C_j) F(x,t)$.
  Consequently, $m_j(x) := min_(t in cal(V)_j(x)) F(x,t)$ is continuous on $U$ and therefore on $cal(X)_"reg"$.
]

#pf[
  Fix $x^0 in cal(X)_"reg"$ and $j$.

  Because $x^0$ is regular, all centers are in edge interiors and the component decomposition is locally stable: there exists a neighborhood $U_0$ of $x^0$ in which the same component labels persist and each $cal(V)_j(x)$ differs from $cal(V)_j(x^0)$ only by small boundary slivers near the moving centers.

  Since $K(r) -> infinity$ as $r arrow.b 0$, points sufficiently close to any center have arbitrarily large potential value.
  Choose $M > m_j(x^0)+1$. By continuity of $F$, after shrinking to a neighborhood $U subs U_0$ we can ensure:
  + every point in those boundary slivers has value $F(x,t) > M$ for all $x in U$,
  + there exists a nonempty compact interior core $C_j subs cal(V)_j(x^0)$ with $F(x,t) <= M-1$ for some $t in C_j$ and all $x in U$.

  Therefore no minimizer of $F(x,dot)$ on $cal(V)_j(x)$ can lie in the slivers, so every minimizer lies in the common compact set $C_j$. Hence
  $
    m_j(x) = min_(t in cal(V)_j(x)) F(x,t) = min_(t in C_j) F(x,t)
  $
  for all $x in U$.

  Now apply the previous theorem to the continuous map $(x,t) mapsto F(x,t)$ on $U times C_j$ with fixed compact feasible set $C_j$. It follows that $x mapsto min_(t in C_j) F(x,t)$ is continuous on $U$, hence $m_j$ is continuous on $U$.

  Since $x^0$ and $j$ were arbitrary, $m_j$ is continuous on all of $cal(X)_"reg"$. $square$
]

#cor[
  In the regular regime, continuity of valley heights can be reduced to a fixed-compact-set minimization problem. No global hemicontinuity argument for the full moving component correspondence is needed.
]

